00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.250
right in the tool.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.410
So with that out
of the way, I do

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.010
need to spend just a little bit
of time defining serverless.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.330
One of the buzzwords in
the title that I used

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.840
is serverless.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.870
And I really believe that there
are real and profound reasons

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:02.970
why you would want to
use serverless today when

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.970
writing new applications or
maybe even taking existing

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.890
applications to the cloud.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:11.130
From an operational model,
there are no services

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:15.180
to provision, no cluster to set
up, no cluster to administer

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.320
or secure.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.490
And the important
thing is that you

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:23.220
are billed for the actual usage
via optimized and transparent

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.690
auto scaling.

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.760
That's the operational model.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.220
From a developer's
perspective, this

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:35.550
is really focused on consuming
and exposing services.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:39.130
It's a very service-focused
environment.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:42.240
You're also reacting
and producing events.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.930
The loose coupling between your
modules, between your services,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.600
is probably done
through eventing.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.850
And finally, we'd
like, as developers,

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.190
probably to have an environment
that's as open as possible

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.630
so we're not locked into
any particular solution.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:02.370
Now, let's go back to
the word serverless.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.050
Many jokes are about serverless.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.550
Yes, there are servers.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:10.110
This is not Google's new and
latest serverless data center.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.970
But there's sometimes
some confusion,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:16.590
and it's kind of weird to
define an area by what it's not,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.150
you know, serverless.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:24.120
So let me suggest that we try to
find a new word, a better word.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.830
How about no-ops?

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.460
I mean, this term is
trying to capture the fact

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.600
that developers can focus
on delivering great apps

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.890
and focus on code, rather
than on the infrastructure.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.410
So who thinks here
this is a good, better

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.930
approach than serverless?

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.110
A few hands.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:46.050
OK.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.990
I think I liked it before
I really realized that this

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.920
is not a whole lot better.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.010
Well, first of all,
here's another term

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.000
that's defining something
by what it is not.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.610
But actually, if you
consider operations

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.730
as the practice of
keeping the tech that

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.430
runs your business
going, then no-ops

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.380
is not really a good term.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.630
So implying that operations is
something that might go away

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:14.790
or is something
that you don't need

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.940
is, I think, a misunderstanding
of ops in general,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.760
and maybe DevOps in particular.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.830
So I'm sorry, I don't
have a better term.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.510
So we're stuck with serverless.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.380
What I do have for you are
some of the best practices

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:33.720
we've developed at
Google over the years.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.610
But before we get
into that, let's

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.520
talk about the
challenges, specifically

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.980
in the context of
serverless when

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.210
it has to do with managing
those serverless workloads

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:49.170
and what makes it a bit
trickier than other workloads.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:52.740
Well first, by definition,
you don't have access

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.000
to the servers.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.640
You cannot install your favorite
agents on the machines to call

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.080
home to some administration
or to some service providing

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.220
monitoring.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.790
There is no SSHing
into the machine,

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.790
and that's a good thing.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:11.820
We don't want you to
log into this machine

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.580
and start creating snowflakes.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.700
The second thing is that
this is an environment that

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.760
has multiple services
interacting with one another,

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.360
or with service buses,
or through messages.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.730
And microservices
are great because you

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:31.200
can operate or scale each
service independently,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.690
but tracing calls
through the system

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:40.110
can be both more critical and
trickier to actually achieve.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.360
And things like
cascading failures

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.760
become a lot harder to diagnose.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.330
Third, cloud and serverless
work really best when

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.250
auto scaling is implemented.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.520
And auto scaling is all
about spinning up instances

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:02.310
to take the load but also taking
them down so we save on cost.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.310
And that's great, but it
increases the overall system's

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.940
entropy and makes monitoring
somewhat more complicated,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.930
because the system
keeps on changing shape.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.830
And finally, serverless
compute workloads are often

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.760
event triggered.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:21.780
So the asynchronous
nature of events

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.720
makes it more complex to
understand how an application

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.450
got into a certain state.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.310
So we do have a
number of challenges

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.760
here specific to serverless
when it comes to monitoring,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.460
managing your environment.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.540
Now let me take a moment to
go through the GCP serverless

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:45.070
compute options to get
everyone on the same page.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:47.820
First of all, we
have Cloud Functions.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:50.280
This is functions
as a service where

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:56.370
you deploy a short amount of
code, a list of dependencies,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:01.150
and the event that
triggers this code to run.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.000
We have App Engine,
which is more

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.980
suited for front
end applications

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.520
with multiple services,
multiple modules.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.030
And this one comes with built
in versioning and traffic

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:13.110
splitting.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.590
And finally, if
you'd rather have

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:21.150
the flexibility and the freedom
of container-based applications

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:24.330
where you can choose
your stack, anything

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.250
you'd like to put
in that container

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.970
and still want the
serverless benefits,

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:33.300
well then, Cloud Run is the new
product we're announcing today

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.520
that has several
sessions that will

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.530
go into the details of this.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.050
But we will dive a
little bit into it

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:43.950
and certainly do a little
demo of monitoring Cloud Run.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:47.190
So Cloud Functions is really
there to react to events.

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.750
And those events can be as
simple as HTTP requests,

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.550
but they can be also
uploaded to a bucket,

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.780
messages posted to
a pub/sub topic.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.300
They could be data
changing in Firestore.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:05.490
They could be metadata changing
in an object in cloud storage.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.900
And there are many more coming.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:09.360
It has all the
serverless qualities

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.680
discussed a second ago, and it
offers a choice of run times.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.150
And as you might see here, we're
adding new modern run times,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.910
updating existing ones,
and adding new ones.

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.980
In particular here,
we're announcing

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:28.560
this week the support
for Java 8 for Functions.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.110
App Engine, as I mentioned, has
been around for a little while

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:34.770
and is a solution to
deploy code as well

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:37.770
and have the cloud scale
the application for you

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.110
based on the load.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.430
There are no clusters
to set up or manage,

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:45.360
and it comes with traffic
splitting, multiple versions

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.330
of your app, and it offers
a wide variety of run times

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.720
as well.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:54.180
This week we're announcing Ruby
2.5 as a new supported runtime,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.280
and there's a dedicated
session for that.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.200
And there should
be links to those

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.940
later in this presentation.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:03.150
And as announced
earlier this morning,

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.100
we also now have
this thing called

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.210
Cloud Run, a fully
managed compute platform

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:12.660
that enables you to run
stateless containers that are

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.550
invocable via HTTP requests.

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:21.870
Cloud Run is serverless, which
means that it extracts away

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.210
all infrastructure
management so you

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.110
can focus on building great
apps with any technology

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.970
stack you'd like.

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.580
It is built with
Knative, letting

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.659
you choose your containers from
either a fully managed Cloud

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:40.360
Run or a Google
Kubernetes Engine

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:42.565
cluster with Cloud Run on GKE.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:48.910
So if you're still confused
about the various options

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:52.180
we have to offer,
think of it this way.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.760
You can think of it in
terms of which artifact

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:58.690
you'd like to give us to run
in this serverless fashion.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:01.420
If it's a function or a
collection of functions,

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.170
well, Cloud Function
clearly is the option

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:04.810
that you should look at.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.540
If you'd like to provide
something that's more granular,

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.760
that has multiple modules
that have dependencies,

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:13.450
well, consider App Engine,
because what you're

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:15.790
giving us is really an app.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.620
And if you'd like to
give us a container

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:20.050
because you want total freedom
in terms of what you're

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:23.800
using inside that
container, well, Cloud Run

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.850
now becomes an amazingly
interesting option.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:31.300
Let me just mention, to add to
the serverless compute picture,

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.160
Cloud Pub/Sub, Cloud
Tasks, and Cloud Scheduler.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.070
These offer very
elegant solutions

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.590
to actually use multiple
products together

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:44.710
if you'd like to orchestrate
several functions

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:48.730
or have different parts being
built in your application

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:52.510
using those different
technologies.

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.600
Now let's go back here
to Cloud Functions,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.640
and let's get into a
small demo and see,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.150
in the DevOps and serverless
topic of this talk,

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:06.310
how you can actually have
a logging set of features

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:07.930
to enable you to
see what's going

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:12.680
on in a serverless and a
Cloud Function application.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.745
So here-- let me go back.

00:10:15.745 --> 00:10:16.245
Oops.

00:10:16.245 --> 00:10:17.035
My apologies.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.680
This is Cloud
Console, and this is

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:27.790
an application in which I can
choose from a set of pictures.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:31.015
And I can say, "Hello
Next Attendees."

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:38.660
And what this will do is
post a message to Pub/Sub

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:40.880
to which four different
functions written

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:45.320
in four different languages
will react and manipulate

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:49.950
that image by adding logos and
the text that I just entered.

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:52.850
Now, if you look at
the back end of this

00:10:52.850 --> 00:10:55.370
and you look at the
console, everything here

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.960
is written with Cloud Functions.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:58.940
We have the few
helper functions.

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:03.720
And we have one that processes
the image in the Go language,

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.420
another one in Java, a couple
in Node, and one in Python.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:14.770
So if I look at, let's say,
this Cloud Function, I can see,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.800
in the course of 24 hours,
how many invocations came

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.401
in-- and there
weren't that many--

00:11:20.401 --> 00:11:23.630
how much time these
took on the average,

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:27.590
and I can see the
various data that's here.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:32.090
And I can, of course,
look at something

00:11:32.090 --> 00:11:34.088
over the course of
two days, for example.

00:11:34.088 --> 00:11:35.630
And what's interesting
here is that I

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.260
can get to the logs of
that specific function.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:42.950
I can see all the
logs aggregated here.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:45.470
I might have multiple
instances of this running.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.720
This might be running on
physically different servers.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:48.950
I really don't have to care.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:52.730
This is a unified,
consolidated, fully managed view

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.980
of the environment.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:56.880
I can filter by log level.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.630
In this case, we only have
two, which are info, typically

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:04.020
the things that I put out
in the standard output,

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:08.160
and the system events, which
are-- the function completed,

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.640
this is how long it took,
or, the function started.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.200
And these are Cloud
Function events.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.950
Now what I can do here is
look at this guy, which is

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.470
what we call the execution ID.

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:27.920
And I would like to show all the
matching entries, so every log

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:33.110
for this function, the
Node function, which

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:34.970
has this execution.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:38.120
So typically, this is
one request for Node.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.490
So I could see
the entire process

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:44.300
starting with the function
starting, the request

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:48.020
ID, the name of the
file, the output file,

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:52.260
downloading files
from compute storage,

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.200
processing the image
using ImageMagick

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:59.030
to do the transformation,
and eventually storing

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.070
the new image, and finishing.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:02.460
Now, that's really nice.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:05.578
You can see here the filter
that was populated for me.

00:13:05.578 --> 00:13:07.370
Maybe what I would like
to do at this point

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:09.770
is remove the fact
that I'm looking

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:12.620
at this Node feature
and this Node function

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:16.340
and actually look
at all the ones

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.920
that have this
specific execution ID.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.500
So I can see that
for one event, I

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:25.430
have a Node, which we just saw
a function executing, a Node

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:28.580
10, a Go function,
a Java function,

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.940
as well as a Python function.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:32.820
And if I take this one
little step further,

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:36.830
I can also see things such
as how long they took.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:44.240
So if I add this "execution
took" additional filter here,

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.930
I could see that, indeed,
I had five functions,

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.690
and they took anywhere between
a few hundred milliseconds

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:51.830
to a few seconds.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:59.770
So that's a quick
demo of what you

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:05.080
can expect in Cloud Functions
when it comes to logging.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:10.450
Of course, there's
way more to logging

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:12.430
and to DevOps than
just logging, and we'll

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:13.570
get into that in a second.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:22.730
Oh, and this demo is
available on the show floor

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:24.830
if you'd like to play
with it and understand

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:28.880
and talk into greater details
and about how it was built.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:33.620
Now, I said that I did not have
a better term, in serverless,

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:36.560
but what I do have are
some DevOps lessons learned

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.870
from Google that have recently
materialized under the name

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:42.290
SRE.

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:46.200
So SRE stands for site
reliability engineering.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.240
And in fact, you can
state the following--

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:51.790
and the developers in the
room will understand this--

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:54.290
SRE implements DevOps.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:58.393
SRE is really an opinionated
implementation of DevOps.

00:14:58.393 --> 00:14:59.935
There are other
valid implementations

00:14:59.935 --> 00:15:03.850
of DevOps principles,
Google just uses SRE.

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:08.080
When you say DevOps,
we really think SRE.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.530
In fact, the industry seems
to have adopted SRE as a term,

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:15.220
and companies such as Microsoft,
Apple, Twitter, Facebook,

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:19.150
Dropbox, Amazon, and
others all have SRE teams

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:20.660
that they call that way.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.880
So let's dive into this
and see what, really,

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:26.000
there is behind this acronym.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:29.540
At Google, we deliver
services to billions of users.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:32.440
And if you look
at those services,

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:38.045
they're really managed by
a fairly small set of SREs.

00:15:38.045 --> 00:15:39.545
We have multiple
products that serve

00:15:39.545 --> 00:15:43.240
a billion or more users
each, and all of them

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:45.850
have a few SREs.

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:49.540
And if you do the math,
that's hundreds of thousands

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:52.990
of users per single SRE.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:55.330
And if you've ever
carried a pager

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:58.550
and been in the ops world,
this is very different.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:02.920
You cannot be pulled into
hundreds or thousands or tens

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:05.800
or hundreds of thousands of
directions trying to put out

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:11.860
fires between the different
projects that you manage.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:17.720
Instead, the SRE model
can keep your users happy,

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:22.120
your business running without
blowing your operations budget.

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:26.650
You cannot scale the number of
SREs with the number of users

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:30.070
that you have, or else you would
be burning out a small group

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:33.940
of people, or you'd be relying
on some heroic individual

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:38.780
actions, which is really
not the point of SREs.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.260
So SREs really, at
the end of the day,

00:16:41.260 --> 00:16:44.140
try to balance two
competing needs.

00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:46.380
The first one is reliability.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.980
Is my service available?

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:54.940
Is it returning 200 error codes,
or is it three, four, or five

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:56.350
errors?

00:16:56.350 --> 00:17:00.910
Second, agility-- you can make
something almost perfectly

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:04.520
stable if you never
allow changes.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:07.810
But Google is really about
experimenting, speed,

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:09.470
and innovation.

00:17:09.470 --> 00:17:14.589
So how do SREs balance both
needs and keep their sanity?

00:17:14.589 --> 00:17:18.052
So the answer lies
really in an iceberg.

00:17:18.052 --> 00:17:19.344
It all starts with the culture.

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:25.910
There are a number of books that
have been written by Googlers,

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:27.710
and I encourage
you to read those.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:28.798
Just Google them.

00:17:28.798 --> 00:17:29.840
They're available online.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:32.720
They're also available
from O'Reilly.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.960
The goal of an SRE is really
to automate him or herself

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:37.530
out of their job.

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:38.990
The engineering
reliability needs

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:41.220
to be built into the
product, and those SREs

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:44.540
participate in the
development of the product.

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.580
And in particular,
they try to make things

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:49.172
as observable as possible.

00:17:49.172 --> 00:17:50.630
One important thing
to note here is

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:53.900
that they can refuse to carry
the pager because they consider

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:58.300
the app or the service to
not be well instrumented.

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:02.060
SREs are also responsible for
on-calls but also for things

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.720
like blameless post-mortems,
which is really an important

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:09.200
part of the SRE culture, but
also things like incident

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:12.050
management, testing, CI/CD--

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:15.660
continuous integration,
continuous deployment.

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:18.380
The other part here
that's really important

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:20.590
is the infrastructure.

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:24.350
SREs are completely outnumbered
by software engineers,

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:28.100
so opinionated infrastructure
is the only approach.

00:18:28.100 --> 00:18:31.260
Every service needs to have
a name to be discoverable,

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.830
to have quota, out
to have permissions,

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:38.210
and it needs to come with
base telemetry no matter what.

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.730
So that platform, that
infrastructure here,

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.580
brings observability.

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:46.700
It captures the data, typically
in a time series database,

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:49.820
and it makes it
available for querying

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:51.822
and for running analysis.

00:18:51.822 --> 00:18:53.780
Now, there are a number
of open-source projects

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.350
we contribute to,
but what we use

00:18:57.350 --> 00:19:02.300
and we expose to Google Cloud
Platform users is Stackdriver,

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:05.220
which is both the
technology, the platform,

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:08.360
as well as the tools
that sit on top of it.

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:11.720
But really, the tooling
is the tip of the iceberg,

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:15.860
and you really need the
bottom layers for all of this

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:19.100
to be extremely useful.

00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:22.340
Now, going back to the
culture aspect of SREs,

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.520
rather than playing
whack-a-mole all day trying

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:29.720
to put out fires, SREs
look at what impacts

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:33.090
customers and users directly.

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:36.740
So this is called a
service level indicator.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:39.590
Let's take a bad example of SLI.

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:42.660
A CPU goes to 80%.

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:46.850
That is not a good
service level indicator.

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:49.340
It could affect the customer,
but in reality, we've

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:53.180
done the obvious thing of
implementing auto scaling.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:55.160
So at some point,
the problem just

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:58.640
goes away, so there's no
real point in paging somebody

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:00.620
in the middle of the
night because that CPU

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:03.650
has hit that threshold.

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.320
An SLI, a service
level indicator,

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:12.440
is fairly close to what other
people refer to as KPIs,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:16.790
and it really needs to impact
your customer or your business.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:19.670
So obvious examples of that,
and probably better examples,

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:22.490
are things around availability.

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:24.530
In other words, how
many of my requests

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:28.310
are coming back with
200 response codes?

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:31.610
And the second one is latency.

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:34.730
What percentage of
my requests come back

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:38.915
with responses in less
than, say, 300 milliseconds?

00:20:38.915 --> 00:20:40.040
These are two obvious ones.

00:20:40.040 --> 00:20:41.930
Of course, you can come
up with other things

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:46.160
that have impact on your
customer or your business.

00:20:46.160 --> 00:20:51.480
Now, moving on to SLOs, or
service level objectives.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:54.820
These are promises
you make to yourself.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.380
At Google SLOs are actually--
and this might come

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:59.420
as a surprise to some of you--

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:02.960
they're never 100%, mostly
because this would mean

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:04.880
you can't ever change anything.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:09.440
And again, we try to
deploy new features,

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:12.485
try new things out, and
innovate fairly quickly.

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.830
So the interesting thing here is
that when you subtract the SLO

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:24.200
from 100, you get something we
refer to as an error budget,

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:28.500
and this is defined for
a given period of time.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:30.580
This is critical and
a key part of SRE

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:33.290
practices, managing that budget.

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:36.170
If you burn through
your budget, this

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.240
means you really should
stop adding new features

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:41.000
and rolling out new experiments
and go back and work

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:45.410
on your technical debt and
make your system more reliable.

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:50.460
On the other hand, if you
never use your budget,

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:52.790
that means you're not
experimenting enough.

00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:54.500
So we call this a
budget for a reason.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.090
We manage this as a budget.

00:21:56.090 --> 00:21:57.260
We have a budget.

00:21:57.260 --> 00:22:00.248
We actually consume that.

00:22:00.248 --> 00:22:01.790
So the bottom line
is that you really

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:06.710
want to make your service as
reliable as your customers need

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:10.740
and spend your error budget
on adding new features.

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:13.310
So when do you
actually page somebody

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:15.740
in the middle of the
night if you have to?

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:19.340
Well, that's when you're error
budget is dropping rapidly,

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:23.660
and that's really something
you should be monitoring.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:26.200
And finally, SLA,
it's important,

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:28.250
but it's really a
business concept.

00:22:28.250 --> 00:22:32.200
And it's just the
business aspect of SLOs.

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:37.960
It doesn't really fit into
the SRE description of things.

00:22:37.960 --> 00:22:42.120
Now, another best practice
is versioning your services,

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:45.070
and better than
that, actually having

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:49.660
those services
multiple ones of them

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:52.300
to be active at the same time.

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:54.790
If you're able to deploy
multiple active versions

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:58.360
of your services, you could
decide to deploy them, maybe,

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.280
from you're promoted
builds, and you

00:23:00.280 --> 00:23:05.470
can carry the build version all
the way through to deployment.

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:08.290
And in this case,
you can then start

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:12.400
doing very interesting
things, such as A/B testing.

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:15.700
Maybe things like
Canary and Blue/Green

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:18.250
deployments, deploying
a new version

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:21.490
and exposing only a small
subset of your users to it.

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:24.348
And then incrementing
and increasing

00:23:24.348 --> 00:23:26.140
the amount of users
that are exposed to it,

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:28.660
and eventually move to
the entire new version

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:32.540
if all goes well, or roll
back to a previous versions.

00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:34.390
So the ability to
have those versions

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:36.910
opens up a lot of
possibilities when

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:40.780
deploying so that you have
both flexibility and confidence

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:42.117
in your deployments.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:48.290
So there are other
sessions that fall

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:52.190
under CI/CD, which I am not
really talking about here.

00:23:52.190 --> 00:23:55.160
And I encourage you to
attend some of these.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:57.500
There's one today, one
tomorrow, and one on Thursday.

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:04.100
Now let's talk about
Stackdriver, both the platform

00:24:04.100 --> 00:24:06.600
and the tools.

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:10.530
Stackdriver really is the
technology that we use

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:14.580
and that implements that
platform that I talked about.

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:16.920
This is where we
collect, of course, logs,

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:18.870
metrics of all sorts.

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:21.990
This is where we extract errors.

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:25.570
This is where we
infer relationships.

00:24:25.570 --> 00:24:28.710
This is something that we
provide dashboards with.

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:33.120
There's a bunch of tools that
are built in the Stackdriver

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:33.990
offering.

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:37.110
But there's also
a set of APIs that

00:24:37.110 --> 00:24:40.260
are available for
others to build upon.

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:44.450
Google Stackdriver is the
externalization of Google's SRE

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:46.680
best practices in a way.

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:50.550
It has the infrastructure, the
platform, and the observability

00:24:50.550 --> 00:24:54.180
for GCP applications that
I've mentioned before.

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.910
So let's quickly go through
some of these tools,

00:24:56.910 --> 00:25:00.570
and we'll be demoing a
number of these next.

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:05.180
So the built-in
products-- you've already

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:07.340
seen the Logging
in my short demo,

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:11.690
with centralized, fully managed
logs across all products.

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:13.940
I used it to show
Cloud Functions,

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:17.360
but this works equally well
across App Engine and Cloud

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:18.290
Run.

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:20.600
And of course, you look at
all those logs regardless

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:24.710
of the physical location
of the application running.

00:25:24.710 --> 00:25:30.920
Cloud Traces takes the execution
ID you saw in that quick demo

00:25:30.920 --> 00:25:36.710
and takes it a bit farther
by providing a view of calls

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:38.450
across services.

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:41.930
How much time does a call
to cloud storage take?

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:45.560
How long is ImageMagick taking
to manipulate that image?

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:48.890
How long is it before I
can write the response

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:51.745
to cloud storage?

00:25:51.745 --> 00:25:53.120
This is a tool
that will actually

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:55.620
help you compare
different traces,

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:57.960
so it's a very powerful one.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.110
Error Reporting is probably
one of my favorites

00:26:00.110 --> 00:26:05.100
because it's really
easy to setup,

00:26:05.100 --> 00:26:06.710
because there is no setup.

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:09.590
This offers alerting
and summaries

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:10.970
for application errors.

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:16.355
As your application generates
errors, 500 typically errors,

00:26:16.355 --> 00:26:22.550
it writes stack traces
to the standard output.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:25.250
It will capture all of this,
and it will provide you

00:26:25.250 --> 00:26:27.830
a view in which you
can see when was

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:31.160
the first time and the last
time this error occurred,

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:34.460
how often does it occur, and,
of course, the stack trace.

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:36.290
So it will group
those together instead

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:39.860
of showing you too
much data, and I

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:46.010
think it's a very useful and
very easy to take advantage of.

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:48.380
Cloud Profiler
and Cloud Debugger

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:51.470
really take all of
this to the next level

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:54.080
because they bring
what is traditionally

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:58.080
development time tooling
to production workloads.

00:26:58.080 --> 00:27:00.620
So through open-source
and minimal overhead,

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:05.450
Cloud Profiler offers CPU
and memory cost analysis down

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:08.750
to function and method levels.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:12.110
Cloud Debugger lets you
inspect the state of a running

00:27:12.110 --> 00:27:15.950
application with life
snapshots, and it even

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:19.920
lets you add log statements
without a redeploy.

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:23.220
So how often have you redeployed
just to add a log statement?

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:25.280
Well, if you use Cloud
Debugger, you probably

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:27.440
will not have to do this again.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:31.330
And we'll see this in
action in a short moment.

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:35.800
So Stackdriver also
exposes data with APIs.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:41.500
So one can use this to build,
for example, SLO monitoring

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:44.712
dashboards, which are really
important because this

00:27:44.712 --> 00:27:45.670
is where it all starts.

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:48.190
Then you need to have the
tools to drill in to understand

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:49.510
what the problem is.

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:52.240
But the monitoring of
your SLO is probably

00:27:52.240 --> 00:27:55.400
where it all starts.

00:27:55.400 --> 00:28:00.030
So speaking of exposing metrics
via the Stackdriver API,

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:04.288
I would like now to introduce
Daniel Langer from Datadog

00:28:04.288 --> 00:28:05.830
to talk about the
integration they've

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:09.590
done with our new release
product, Cloud Run.

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:11.307
Over to you Daniel.

00:28:11.307 --> 00:28:12.140
DANIEL LANGER: Cool.

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.600
Thank you, Alexis.

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.550
So like Alexis just
said, my name is Daniel.

00:28:16.550 --> 00:28:19.855
I'm a I'm a product
manager at Datadog.

00:28:19.855 --> 00:28:21.230
And really quick
for those of you

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:25.340
who don't know what Datadog is,
we're a monitoring platform.

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:27.620
So we collect
infrastructure metrics,

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:30.300
traces, and application
level metrics, logs.

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:33.230
We centralize it in
one SaaS platform,

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:37.085
let you create alerts,
dashboards, do cause analysis.

00:28:37.085 --> 00:28:38.840
We're an operations
tool to make sure

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:40.298
that everything in
your environment

00:28:40.298 --> 00:28:41.970
is running smoothly.

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:45.753
We have an open-source agent
and over 250 integrations

00:28:45.753 --> 00:28:47.420
and an open API, so
that no matter where

00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:48.878
you're running what
you're running,

00:28:48.878 --> 00:28:52.130
you can monitor
it within Datadog.

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:56.520
We are over 7,500 customers.

00:28:56.520 --> 00:28:58.520
We're processing trillions
of data points a day,

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:03.050
so we've got some
scale behind us.

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:06.860
We've been a partner with
Google Cloud for many years now,

00:29:06.860 --> 00:29:09.140
and we have tons of
different integrations.

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:11.150
So whatever you're
running in Google Cloud,

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:13.580
you can monitor it in Datadog.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:17.930
So we collect metrics via
Stackdriver and other sources.

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:20.510
And today we are
excited to announce

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:23.330
a brand new integration
with Stackdriver logs,

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:25.910
so that whatever logs
you're generating

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:27.470
that are being stored
in Stackdriver,

00:29:27.470 --> 00:29:29.930
you can now view
them in Datadog.

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.960
What's more, with the
announcement of Google Cloud

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:35.210
Run today, you can
both send metrics

00:29:35.210 --> 00:29:39.170
and logs from that service
into Datadog as well.

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:41.480
Like I mentioned, we
also have an agent,

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:43.640
this open-source program
that you can deploy

00:29:43.640 --> 00:29:45.170
where you have servers running.

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:47.390
If you're running
Compute Engine or GKE,

00:29:47.390 --> 00:29:50.270
you can deploy the Datadog
agent right onto those

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:53.270
to get insights into
custom metrics, traces,

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:54.650
and logs as well.

00:29:57.380 --> 00:30:00.850
So really exciting Cloud
Run came out today.

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:05.830
So I want to do a quick demo
of how you can monitor this

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:06.520
within Datadog.

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:15.300
So I've set up a pretty
simple Cloud Run service,

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:17.910
and I've had a couple
of revisions going.

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:20.550
In this example, I
allocated a low amount

00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:23.570
of memory, a low
amount of concurrency

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.530
just to get it going.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:28.260
Behind the scenes is a
super simple Flask app.

00:30:28.260 --> 00:30:30.030
I have a couple
routes I've set up--

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:33.960
a "hello world" route, a slash
"Daniel rocks" route, and then

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:38.490
a route that's going to eat up
the memory of this Cloud Run

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:39.830
service.

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:40.980
So super simple.

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:42.595
And if we go over
to my terminal,

00:30:42.595 --> 00:30:44.220
we can see that I
have a script running

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:47.370
that's hitting the "hello
world" and the "Daniel" route.

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:49.150
It's running smoothly.

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:52.660
It's fetching over and over
again, nice and smooth.

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:54.707
And if we pop on
over to Datadog,

00:30:54.707 --> 00:30:56.290
I want to show you
a couple of things.

00:30:56.290 --> 00:31:01.860
So what this is a
Datadog time board

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:05.790
where you can drag and drop
widgets and plot metrics

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:08.010
and information across
your entire infrastructure.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:09.930
And in this one, we
have a few things.

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:13.400
You can see that we have 200
responses for this revision.

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:14.700
Everything is looking nice.

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:18.120
Our latency is smooth, and
our CPU and memory allocation

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:19.950
are pretty constant as well.

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:23.450
So as this is running, you
can see that the service

00:31:23.450 --> 00:31:26.230
is performing nicely.

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:27.880
But let's throw a
wrench into that.

00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:30.510
So let's go back to
terminal, and let's

00:31:30.510 --> 00:31:33.580
run this eat memory
script that's going

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:36.000
to hit that other endpoint.

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:37.808
And as we can see,
it's now spitting out

00:31:37.808 --> 00:31:38.850
that we're out of memory.

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:42.290
This is a print statement
that I actually made.

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:44.040
If we hop on over back
to my other script,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:45.930
we can see it's
acting up as well.

00:31:45.930 --> 00:31:48.870
Hitting this new endpoint
that's eating up memory

00:31:48.870 --> 00:31:52.180
is causing things to
get a little funky.

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:56.220
So if we hop on
back over to Datadog

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:58.830
and we take a look at
our updated dashboard,

00:31:58.830 --> 00:32:02.130
we can see this in Datadog.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.530
The number of 200 has gone down.

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:07.430
The request latency has gone up.

00:32:07.430 --> 00:32:08.950
And in this case, we know why.

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:13.560
I started running
the script that's

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:15.870
hitting this eat memory thing.

00:32:15.870 --> 00:32:18.820
But let's say you were
the owner for this service

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:22.410
and you had no idea why this
request latency was going up.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:24.870
You just saw this graph,
or you received an alert

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:27.350
that latency had gone up.

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:28.370
What do you do?

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:31.250
Well, this is where the
power of logs comes in,

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:33.740
and especially
important to understand

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:35.880
the "why" something's happening.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:37.670
So what we can do
is, right in Datadog,

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:40.160
we can click on that
spike, and we can go down

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:42.320
to view related logs.

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:44.090
And what that's going
to do is take us

00:32:44.090 --> 00:32:48.830
to the Datadog Log Explorer
scoped to that exact time frame

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:50.210
with the context in frame.

00:32:50.210 --> 00:32:51.710
So we can see we
have a three minute

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:54.470
window of logs right
before and after I clicked,

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:58.250
and its scoped to that
revision that that time series

00:32:58.250 --> 00:32:59.990
plot referred to.

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:01.740
So we have a nice scoping here.

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:04.850
However, as you can see, we
still have over 1,000 log lines

00:33:04.850 --> 00:33:07.520
for those couple minutes,
so it's still pretty broad.

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:09.300
But we can quickly
narrow that down.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:11.470
We can say let's only
look at the error logs.

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:12.470
So we've scoped it down.

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:13.680
We only have 90 now.

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:16.100
And we could also use a
feature called Patterns, which

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:18.640
will automatically
group similar logs,

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:21.482
so that it's easy to find
needles in a haystack.

00:33:21.482 --> 00:33:23.690
In this case, we only have
a couple of types of logs,

00:33:23.690 --> 00:33:24.920
so it's not super exciting.

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:28.670
But now we can quickly see
that the cause of our issue

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:29.913
is we're hitting that memory.

00:33:29.913 --> 00:33:31.580
Not super exciting,
we knew this is why.

00:33:31.580 --> 00:33:34.190
But if you were a developer
trying to debug it,

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:37.340
this would be
really, really vital.

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:41.190
It will automatically parse out
important attributes from logs.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:44.090
So you can easily see them,
create facets, search for them,

00:33:44.090 --> 00:33:45.240
query for them.

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:46.950
Whatever might be
important to you,

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:50.500
you can search for
them in Datadog Logs.

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:55.690
I want to quickly go back to the
Datadog dashboard really quick

00:33:55.690 --> 00:33:57.910
and highlight one
thing that Alexis

00:33:57.910 --> 00:33:59.890
touched on that's
really important,

00:33:59.890 --> 00:34:02.160
and that is SLOs and SLIs.

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:06.430
So this widget you see here
is called the Datadog Uptime

00:34:06.430 --> 00:34:10.340
widget, and it lets you track
those exact things, those SLOs,

00:34:10.340 --> 00:34:11.590
right in Datadog.

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:14.830
So in this example, we have
an application latency.

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:16.760
That's our service
level indicator.

00:34:16.760 --> 00:34:19.190
And we see over
different time periods

00:34:19.190 --> 00:34:20.920
if we are meeting our SLOs.

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:24.159
Sadly, in this example, it
doesn't appear that we are.

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:29.080
So alongside your service
metrics, application metrics,

00:34:29.080 --> 00:34:31.360
you can understand
your SLOs as well.

00:34:34.170 --> 00:34:36.469
So that was just a brief
foray into a Datadog

00:34:36.469 --> 00:34:38.420
and what we're doing
with Cloud Run already.

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:41.094
We are super excited
by the launch today.

00:34:41.094 --> 00:34:42.469
We expect great
things, and we're

00:34:42.469 --> 00:34:45.370
looking forward to learning
more as you all begin to use it.

00:34:45.370 --> 00:34:48.110
I'm going to pass it back
on over to Alexis now.

00:34:48.110 --> 00:34:48.861
Thank you.

00:34:48.861 --> 00:34:52.228
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:54.942 --> 00:34:57.400
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Thank you, Daniel, for the demo.

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:59.310
Thank you for the plug for SLOs.

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:01.800
I think it's really important
to be monitoring those SLOs.

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:05.100
That's really something that's
been working well for us.

00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:08.860
And also, there was no
set up on the client side.

00:35:08.860 --> 00:35:11.640
The people writing
the applications

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:15.440
didn't have to integrate with
Datadog or with Stackdriver.

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:18.430
Again, this is something
that you get for free,

00:35:18.430 --> 00:35:20.490
and then you have, of
course, a number of tools

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:23.090
that you can use
to monitor things.

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:25.500
Now let's switch gears
a little bit and talk

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:26.790
about the Cloud Debugger.

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:30.367
And for that, let me introduce
Ludovic, a software engineer

00:35:30.367 --> 00:35:31.950
working on an App
Engine standard, who

00:35:31.950 --> 00:35:35.130
will walk you through some
demos on App Engine and Cloud

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:36.070
Debugger.

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:37.070
LUDOVIC CHAMPENOIS: Yep.

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:38.020
Thank you, Alexis.

00:35:38.020 --> 00:35:44.330
So we'll try to do a demo
of the Cloud Debugger.

00:35:44.330 --> 00:35:45.860
Where is-- OK.

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:47.300
I see it.

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:57.070
So to run the Cloud Debugger
in an App Engine application,

00:35:57.070 --> 00:36:01.660
we will run an application
called Pic-a-Daily,

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:04.240
which can upload--

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:09.140
well, let's switch to the
second laptop, please.

00:36:09.140 --> 00:36:12.050
So it's pic-a-daily.appspot.com.

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:16.700
And you can upload a
picture, and we scan it,

00:36:16.700 --> 00:36:18.140
and we tried to find tags.

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:22.020
So here, this picture
was taken yesterday,

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:26.870
and we can see it's a vehicle,
a car, a road, lane, parking,

00:36:26.870 --> 00:36:28.020
street, whatever.

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:28.520
OK.

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:32.660
Or a bowl of strawberries,
we can find it's strawberries

00:36:32.660 --> 00:36:36.270
using Google Cloud Vision APIs.

00:36:36.270 --> 00:36:40.220
So this application is a
collection of microservices.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:43.710
The front end is written in Java
as an App Engine application.

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:47.780
So here you see the
App Engine console

00:36:47.780 --> 00:36:50.560
with the current
service running.

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:53.120
And it's running on Java 8.

00:36:53.120 --> 00:36:57.185
So these are the
list of applications

00:36:57.185 --> 00:36:58.185
that have been deployed.

00:36:58.185 --> 00:36:59.420
So it's a Java application.

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:02.090
You deploy it as a web app.

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:07.010
And it would be
nice if you could

00:37:07.010 --> 00:37:09.830
look at all the logs
of this application

00:37:09.830 --> 00:37:13.290
even after this application
has been deployed.

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:15.150
OK, so for that,
we're going to use

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:19.550
a tool which is fully
integrated with App Engine.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:23.080
So if I go to the Tools
menu, you have two views.

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:25.630
One is Log, and one is Debug.

00:37:25.630 --> 00:37:28.870
So the Debug view will
switch the Cloud Console

00:37:28.870 --> 00:37:33.370
to the Cloud Debugger,
which is configured

00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:37.060
for you out of the box for all
the App Engine applications.

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:40.580
So here, to debug
an application,

00:37:40.580 --> 00:37:42.720
you need source code usually.

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:44.110
And what you deploy
to App Engine

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:47.950
is JAR file or a collection
of JARs and frameworks.

00:37:47.950 --> 00:37:52.970
So to make the console
aware of your source code,

00:37:52.970 --> 00:37:55.150
you can either connect
your application

00:37:55.150 --> 00:38:00.940
with a cloud-sourced resource,
a cloud-sourced repository,

00:38:00.940 --> 00:38:07.910
or you can upload a
file from your laptop.

00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:12.130
The only thing we need is source
code to map to a line number

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:14.000
in a dodge .java file.

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:17.890
So here the App
Engine application

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:20.790
has been connected to
a cloud repository,

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:24.340
and I can now navigate
to Java source code.

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:28.810
So you can see we are using
cloud APIs for storage.

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.370
We are using a Spark
Java framework,

00:38:31.370 --> 00:38:35.030
which is really cool.

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:37.870
You can do two things
with this Cloud Debugger.

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:41.670
You can set a breakpoint, like
you would do on your laptop,

00:38:41.670 --> 00:38:44.210
for local debugging.

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:47.620
And it's very interesting
because in the cloud

00:38:47.620 --> 00:38:52.100
an App Engine application can
scale to millions of JVMs.

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:55.720
OK, so which one do you
attach a debugger to?

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:57.260
It's quite complicated.

00:38:57.260 --> 00:39:01.090
So the Cloud
Debugger will listen

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:04.720
to a snapshot, which are
more or less breakpoints,

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:07.490
without stopping
your application.

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:10.750
And the first JVM
reaching this line

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:14.110
will capture the
state of the memory

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:17.420
and dump it somewhere so
that you can analyze it.

00:39:17.420 --> 00:39:20.320
So it is very cool,
because let's say

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:22.280
I want to set a breakpoint--

00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:26.430
not here-- in line--

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:29.630
So here we have a
collection of pictures

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:32.770
here, which is an array list.

00:39:32.770 --> 00:39:36.010
We populate it, so let's
put a breakpoint after.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:37.890
So I just click on
the line number--

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:41.650
well, snapshot and line number.

00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:45.380
And now the debugger [INAUDIBLE]
is waiting for the application

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:46.260
to be triggered.

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:49.264
So let's reload the application.

00:39:49.264 --> 00:39:52.580
Oh, somebody took a picture.

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:55.330
And now, maybe
we'll see it here,

00:39:55.330 --> 00:39:59.170
because in this
debugger view, I can

00:39:59.170 --> 00:40:02.530
see the state of the
memory of my JVM.

00:40:02.530 --> 00:40:08.450
So I can look at all the
variables-- request, response,

00:40:08.450 --> 00:40:11.420
the pictures, which
is an array list.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:14.360
And the first one,
that might have

00:40:14.360 --> 00:40:17.920
been taken right away with
a call-- or two minutes ago.

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:18.830
OK.

00:40:18.830 --> 00:40:22.760
So I can debug live my
application in production

00:40:22.760 --> 00:40:24.440
with the Cloud Debugger.

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:28.970
So you have seen in the previous
presentation all the logging

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:32.480
capabilities of Google Cloud.

00:40:32.480 --> 00:40:35.960
It would be nice if, after you
deploy your Java application,

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:40.470
to inject a new log entry
point in your log viewer.

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:40.970
OK.

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:44.640
So Cloud Debugger can do that
with what we call a log point.

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:46.400
So instead of
setting a breakpoint

00:40:46.400 --> 00:40:53.450
to your application, what you
want to add after deployment

00:40:53.450 --> 00:40:54.650
is an entry in your log.

00:40:54.650 --> 00:41:02.600
So here in line 184, I
would type an expression,

00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:08.040
which is the content I want
to add every time this line is

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:09.900
reached in my application.

00:41:09.900 --> 00:41:12.000
So here I can put
some text or pictures.

00:41:18.741 --> 00:41:20.210
OK.

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:24.090
"Pic equals--" and
to put a variable,

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:27.209
you put open brackets,
so "pictures.get(0).

00:41:33.770 --> 00:41:38.570
So I will just dump
the first picture,

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:43.440
the first entry in the array.

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:46.800
Sorry, I put a typo,
so I can edit it here.

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:47.680
Good point.

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:50.220
I'm very bad at typing.

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:50.820
Thank you.

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:52.200
You are debugging my debugger.

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:54.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:54.350 --> 00:41:56.390
I apply, and then--

00:41:56.390 --> 00:42:01.520
so apparently the log
point has been entered.

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:08.500
So let's reload the
application here.

00:42:08.500 --> 00:42:11.540
OK, another picture.

00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:17.800
If I go back to the debugger,
I set the log level to Info,

00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:21.490
so now in my log
viewer in the console,

00:42:21.490 --> 00:42:24.645
I should find somewhere--

00:42:24.645 --> 00:42:26.020
I don't know
exactly where it is.

00:42:26.020 --> 00:42:28.226
But I should find--

00:42:28.226 --> 00:42:32.016
we'll load more, or reload--

00:42:32.016 --> 00:42:38.420
yeah here, my new
line in the log,

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:41.700
which can be now analyzed
with all the other log

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:44.200
viewers and Stackdriver
capabilities

00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:46.510
of our integration.

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:51.410
So this is, in a few minutes,
a demo of the Cloud Debugger

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.540
running live in App Engine.

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:56.810
So it's enabled by
default. It has zero cost.

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:59.280
It doesn't slow down
your application.

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:00.830
And when you use
it, it's very handy

00:43:00.830 --> 00:43:04.930
because we all make mistakes,
and we need debuggers,

00:43:04.930 --> 00:43:07.602
even in production.

